    "updated_at": "2026-10-02T13:06:11Z",
    "body": "> It suggests that the `:visited` wording is wrong as the existing protections are at the `getComputedStyle()` level whereas we need them at the painting level here.\r\n\r\nThis is right, the mitigation isn't the same. The wording I used was:\r\n\r\n> This specification does not specify how to preserve user privacy, but user agents must align with what they do in other contexts, like style recalculation and selector matching.\r\n\r\nBy \"align with\" I mean that a script that uses `getComputedStyle()` or `elm.matches(':visited')` should observe the same state as a script that reads pixel colors after `drawElementImage()`. In other words, there shouldn't be a different [used value](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/#used-value) observable via paint.\r\n\r\nIn the spirit of CSS spec's flexibility here, I think I'll just say \"must preserve user privacy\" and have a note pointing out that the mitigations already applied for CSS might not be enough.",
